(project pricklypear-tree-time
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: tree-time — the generation axis on the one tree. Versioned leaves keyed (branch, path, gen); history, undo, and as-of reads become storage semantics instead of per-purpose scaffolding tables. Subsumes 012 code-history, gives local-first sync its merge clock, and closes the beads-audit-gap 'data state history: NO / time travel: NO' rows. Apps stay two-dimensional: every read defaults to a slice at head.")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-09 design discussion, three sessions of prose: (1) app-level undo in notes — notes has one write funnel (notes/doc/upsert) over pure outline transforms, making it the ideal first consumer; per-note before-images. (2) history-as-tree-of-nodes under an h.* prefix — workable without kernel change but pure copying. (3) the answer that stuck: make generation a first-class storage key on the tree itself. Geometry: three overlaid trees — path-space within a branch, branch-as-parent-pointer tree across the world, gen chains within each branch. A branch is a named fork in path-space; a gen-fork (undo + write again) is an anonymous fork in time-space. The gen tree branches exactly when undo is followed by a new write — redo and lost-timeline retention fall out of keeping both children. Prior art cross-read: beads-audit-gap note (Yegge's Beads = Dolt versioning + events + interactions + prose intent). Our answer is more elegant because the habitat collapses the stack: Dolt reimplemented a storage engine and a database; we have Postgres; Beads bolted audit on a CLI; our evaluator sits inside the store. Dolt's advantages are operationalizing (shipped GC, sync wire protocol, scale-tested diff) — each already has a named home here (jobs runner + gen-range partitions, local-first.sync manifest exchange, tree-diff merge). Downstream blast radius audit: libs/* prim surface unchanged (new optional arg only); scaffolding tables stay head-shaped mirrors; 012 code_history is RETIRED (subsumed); 017 branch_generations is promoted from cache-invalidation signal to head coordinate; mirror triggers gain one head-gen filter; the real kernel work is tree_store.ml (insert-only + head bump) + crud.ml/prims_db.ml (gen filter, optional :gen arg) + one view rewrite. Named trees-hash-consing, trees-share-nodes-not-blobs as future notes on this chapter, per one-tree.bl-blast-radius precedent. 5.0 plan pass (campaign-v1): geometry + versioned-leaves + projections + reads-as-of-gen all designed above; migration-018 subsection pins the 018_tree_versions.sql schema contract (5.1 make is deterministic); chapter wired into the book root; all stanzas stay planned (implementation flips land with 5.1/5.2/5.3).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section tree-time

  (doc "Generation as a storage key on the one tree: (branch, path, gen) -> node")
  (status planned)
  (details

   (subsection geometry
    (doc "Three overlaid trees; apps see a 2D slice")
    (status planned)
    (details
     (doc (|
          THREE OVERLAID TREES. The habitat's storage geometry is
          three parent-pointer structures, and every value
          resolution is a walk up one of them:

            1. PATH-SPACE. Within a branch the tree is rooted:
               b.<branch>.* for code lenses, data.u.<user>.* /
               data.shared.* for data (one-tree.tree-model).

            2. BRANCH LINEAGE. Branches form a tree via parent
               pointers (system/main -> admin/personal -> admin/dev),
               with merges as occasional DAG stitches
               (branching.borg). Lookup walks up the branch chain
               until it finds the newest write of a path.

            3. GENERATION. THIS CHAPTER ADDS THE THIRD. Within one
               branch, writes form a gen chain: gen 1 -> gen 2 ->
               gen 3 ... The chain is linear UNTIL undo-and-rewrite
               walks the head pointer back and writes again, at
               which point the gen tree grows a sibling. Redo and
               alternate timelines are properties of this tree, not
               of any undo manager.

          A BRANCH IS A USER-NAMED FORK IN PATH-SPACE; A GEN-FORK IS
          AN ANONYMOUS FORK IN TIME-SPACE. Same mechanism at two
          granularities: branches are expensive, meaningful,
          user-named (fork for an experiment); gens are free,
          continuous, system-named (every write). This self-
          similarity is why ONE three-way tree-diff algorithm
          serves branch merge, gen-axis diff (undo inspection), and
          local-first sync (local-first.sync's manifest exchange is
          the same diff with a second site).

          APPS SEE A 2D SLICE. Every read in the system defaults to
          (current branch or ancestor, path, head gen). No package
          code (libs/*) changes: prim signatures stay
          (get 'note id); the gen axis is opt-in via new optional
          args and new prims. "Present day" is a read policy, not a
          storage property.

          DATA PATHS ARE BRANCH-DEGENERATE. data.u.<user>.* is
          branch-free per the one-tree /data DECISION, so for data
          the branch coordinate collapses and gen is the only real
          second axis. If placement-B (per-branch versioned data)
          ever reopens per its recorded trigger, data becomes
          genuinely 3D and this same storage serves it — no second
          versioning scheme needed.
     |))))

   (subsection versioned-leaves
    (doc "The storage change: tree leaves keyed (branch, path, gen); writes are append")
    (status implemented)
    (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml
                migrations/018_tree_versions.sql
                migrations/019_tree_version_shadow.sql
                migrations/020_tree_version_echo_guard.sql
                scripts/verify_tree_version_shadow.sh)
    (verify
     (smoke "ksh scripts/verify_tree_version_shadow.sh (Phase 1 shadow: trigger present, every shadowed live tree_nodes row's newest version row matches head hash+body, no TOMBSTONE at head for live rows, head counters never behind version rows)")
     (smoke "image/test/tree_version_shadow_test: datum create shadows one row; datum update appends + as-of read returns old body; datum delete appends TOMBSTONE; fn code-path bumps branch_generations"))
    (details
     (doc (|
          NEW TABLE tree_versions, keyed (branch_id, path, gen):

            gen is the tree-scoped (branch-scoped for b.* paths;
            tree-scoped for branch-free data.* paths) monotonic
            counter ALREADY COUNTED by branch_generations (migration
            017) and the tree_generations variant proposed with it.
            017 was built as a cache-invalidation signal for
            remote-eval.caching; this chapter promotes the counter
            to the storage key — same counter, second use, exactly
            as local-first.sync's last-synced merge base is the
            same counter's third use.

          WRITES ARE APPEND. update and delete never rewrite a row:
            - update writes (branch, path, gen+1, new body).
            - delete writes (branch, path, gen+1, TOMBSTONE) — a
              tombstone row is the marker, never a DELETE. This is
              what makes undo-of-delete and audit-by-state-diff
              free.
            - create is identical (the first version row).
          Structural sharing falls out of append-only without
          content addressing: a write touches exactly the changed
          paths; every other path's newest row is untouched. The
          tree's nodes are our chunk granularity — habitat data is
          grain-sized (notes outlines, todos, defs are KB-scale),
          so per-node sharing is near-optimal where Dolt needs
          chunk-level sharing inside large tables. (Hedge recorded:
          a 50MB table-shaped datum updated one field at a time
          pays O(node) per write; the culture says data is grain-
          sized, and content addressing — one-tree.tree-model
          "content-addressing later" — is the physical dedup answer
          if that changes. Hash-chained gens (tamper evidence) are
          NOT required: the image writer is trusted; this chapter
          does not build a Merkle-DAG, it uses hash comparison for
          diff, not integrity.)

          READ PATHS. Leaves may carry precomputed subtree hashes
          (one-tree.tree-model: hash over canonical body, root
          hashes compose) so tree-diff across gens is hash-first:
          equal hash = prune, differ = walk. This is the existing
          subtree-hash plan with a second consumer (the first was
          branching merge; the third is local-first sync).

          MIRROR PRESERVATION — the bl-blast-radius contract
          applies in full: a scaffolding-table writer and a tree
          writer see IDENTICAL history, INCLUDING writes made
          through the other path and from AFTER v1 shipped, or the
          swap corrupts merge bases. The triggers must keep the
          tables and the versioned tree in lockstep both ways, as
          migration 009 established and this chapter extends into
          the time dimension: table UPDATE/DELETE both write a new
          version row AND keep the head-shaped mirror current.
     |))))

   (subsection migration-018
    (doc "The 018_tree_versions.sql schema contract (design, plan pass)")
    (status planned)
    (implements migrations/018_tree_versions.sql)
    (details
     (doc (|
          THIS IS THE PLAN-PASS SCHEMA CONTRACT for
          migrations/018_tree_versions.sql — the file does not
          exist yet; 5.0 designs it here so 5.1 (make
          versioned-leaves) is deterministic, mirroring how 4.0
          pinned 015_jobs.sql in borge/jobs.borg.

          TABLE tree_versions — append-only history, one row per
          (branch_id, path, gen) write:

            branch_id  uuid        NULL for branch-free data.* paths
                                   (otherwise the owning branch);
            path       ltree NOT NULL   (same encoding as tree_nodes:
                                   b.<hex(branch_id)>.fn.<label> /
                                   data.<type_name>.<id>);
            gen        bigint NOT NULL  (the gen counter — see below
                                   for the per-path-class source);
            kind       text NOT NULL    (fn | type | route | datum);
            type_name  text             (datum only);
            body       jsonb NOT NULL   (the node envelope {v,kind,path,
                                   type,body,author,doc,hash};
                                   TOMBSTONE is a reserved body marker
                                   for delete — see below);
            hash       text NOT NULL    (sha256 over canonical body;
                                   precomputed subtree-hash consumer);
            author     text NOT NULL;
            created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()   (write
                                   time; immutable per version row).
            PK (branch_id, path, gen)   — unique per write. branch_id
              NULL allowed only on data.* paths; enforce with a
              CHECK that branch_id IS NOT NULL when path <@ 'b.*'.

          COVERING INDEX for head/projection reads:
              (branch_id, path, gen DESC)
            A b-tree range scan returns the newest gen per path in
            one pass (projections subsection). No join needed at
            habitat scale.

          GEN COUNTER SOURCE, per path class (reads-as-of-gen):
            - code paths (b.<branch>.*): branch_generations
              (migration 017) with its per-branch bump discipline
              IS the head counter; each new version row takes
              branch_generations.generation+1 and the counter
              bumps. The 017 triggers already fire on the
              scaffolding writes bound to these paths.
            - data.* paths: branch-free, so a TREE-WIDE
              tree_generations (bigint) row is the head; every
              data write takes gen+1 (the 017 root-row bump
              discipline generalized to one row). Deliberately NOT
              per-path — under (branch_id, path, gen) the per-path
              order is implicit; the global counter keeps undo /
              diff ordering tree-wide and matches the
              local-first.sync merge clock.

          TOMBSTONE: delete writes (branch_id, path, gen+1, body =
          {v:1,kind,path,type,body:null,author,doc:null,hash:<hash of
          tombstone marker>}) — a row, never a DELETE. A path is
          ALIVE at gen when its newest row at or below head is NOT a
          tombstone. A tombstone row IS the head-shaped mirror's
          delete signal (mirror rows DROP; the history row remains).

          SUBTREE HASH: hash is over the canonical body (as today);
          the chapter's content-addressing note stays deferred — this
          column is the diff-first-prune comparison key, not a Merkle
          chain.

          PARTITIONING for gc (gc subsection): tree_versions is
          declared RANGE PARTITIONED BY list(gen/1000) with lazy
          partition creation; DROP PARTITION is the retention path.
          The 018 migration creates the table + base partition only;
          gen-window partitions are created by the retention worker.

          ALL ADDITIVE per migrations.borg additive-only + image-
          applied-by-image discipline; 018 applies idempotently and
          registers in schema_migrations. Nothing reads this table
          until Phase 2 of coexistence-plan flips the PP_* flag; the
          scaffolding mirrors + tree_nodes remain the source of truth
          through Phase 1 dual-write shadow.
     |))))

   (subsection projections
    (doc "Head-shaped scaffolding stays bit-identical: views gain one predicate; mirrors gain one filter")
    (status planned)
    (implements image/lib/prims_db.ml image/lib/crud.ml migrations/009_tree.sql)
    (details
     (doc (|
          THE VIEWS ARE THE API. Reader concentration from the
          projection era is what this change rides down:

            - tree_data_rows and friends gain one predicate:
              greatest (branch, path) row with gen <= head, and
              no tombstone as newest. A b-tree range scan
              ((branch_id, path, gen DESC) covering index) —
              one index range scan per read, no join, well inside
              the image.borg per-page DB-cost ceilings at habitat
              scale.

            - The scaffolding tables (function_defs, type_defs,
              view_bindings, data_rows) STAY HEAD-SHAPED. Gen
              history lives inside the versioned tree only; the
              mirrors reflect head, as they do today. Every
              residual reader, fork-copy path, admin page, and
              /data browser sees a bit-identical HEAD. Zero package
              code changes.

            - WRITE-then-READ within one request must read its own
              write — crud.ml's in-transaction reads already run on
              the bound connection (Io_lwt.Db.current_conn); the
              head pointer for any in-flight read is the
              transaction's gen, so this property is inherited from
              the existing transaction discipline, not re-derived.

            - The mirror triggers (009 both directions, 020 AST
              projection) gain ONE further condition: head-shaped
              targets refresh only when the versioned write is the
              new head. Append-side, each write is by construction
              the new head of its (branch, path), so the filter is
              trivial; the condition matters for future restore/
              head-move operations.

          WHAT LEAVES: the 012 code_history triggers and table.
          Their job (before-images of code rows at writer-txn
          fidelity) is subsumed: a history read = read the version
          row at gen-1. code-history and code-diff prims KEEP THEIR
          SIGNATURES and re-implement over tree_versions (see
          reads-as-of-gen). The 012 table itself: read-only
          archival during phase 2; dropped in a later migration
          once equivalence is verified (one verifier script:
          sample N names, compare 012 rows' bodies to tree_versions
          bodies at matching changed_at).
     |))))

   (subsection reads-as-of-gen
    (doc "The new prim surface: optional gen coordinate + four new prims")
    (status planned)
    (implements image/lib/prims_db.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
    (details
     (doc (|
          EXISTING PRIMS GAIN A FALLBACK COORDINATE, NOT A REQUIRED
          ONE. get / rows / tree_get take an optional trailing
          :gen N argument; omitted means head. No existing call
          site in libs/* or the kernel changes meaning.

          NEW PRIMS (small surface, all ordinary prim_fns):

            (gen-now)                       -> current tree gen
            (gen-read path gen)             -> node body at gen,
                                               crossing the same
                                               branch lens rules as
                                               head reads
            (gen-diff path gen-a gen-b)     -> node-granular diff:
                                               hashes first, walk
                                               on mismatch (same
                                               algorithm branching
                                               merge + local-first
                                               sync use)
            (undo path) / (undo path n)     -> restore the value at
                                               head-n as a NEW
                                               write at head+1.
                                               Restore-by-write,
                                               not head-pointer
                                               manipulation: the
                                               restore itself is
                                               versioned, so undo is
                                               lossless by the same
                                               argument as
                                               restore-by-redefine
                                               in code-history
                                               (branching.borg).
                                               Redo = (undo path)
                                               again on the
                                               restored state via
                                               the gen chain; no
                                               separate redo stack.

          WHERE THE HEAD POINTER IS, per path class:
            - b.<branch>.* code paths: per-branch — the
              branch_generations counter IS the head. Undo on code
              paths is branch-local: read (branch, path, gen-1),
              write to (branch, path, gen+1) on that branch.
            - data.* paths: branch-free, so the head pointer is
              a tree-wide data generation (the 017 migration's
              root-row bump discipline generalizes to one
              tree_generations row). Undo on data paths is
              per-path: it moves no global pointer, only appends a
              restored value for that one path. NOTHING about
              multi-user safety changes: Andrea's undo of her
              note is an append to (data.u.andrea.note.<id>, gen+1);
              Beth's head is untouched because the head is a
              counter, not per-path state.

          EVAL-SCOPE UNDO IS NOT A PRIM. "Undo my session's last N
          writes" is the habitat-events stream (event journal of
          eval forms) correlated with paths, then a sequence of
          per-path undos — rerun bookkeeping, not storage. This
          chapter deliberately does NOT build cross-path
          transaction-undo; habitat-events + per-path (undo path)
          compose it when both exist.

          COST ACCOUNTING (agent guidance): the first
          gen-as-of read pays one index range scan; the
          authoritative cost playbook applies — gen-diff of large
          subtrees belongs in the read tool you call, not pasted
          wholesale into eval context.
     |))))

   (subsection consumers
    (doc "What this subsumes or feeds, in dependency order")
    (status planned)
    (details
     (doc (|
          SUBSUMES: branching.code-history (012). The separate
          before-image trigger table exists because the tree did
          not keep history. Now it does. code-history / code-diff /
          code-restore become reads of the tree; restore gains a
          real prim (code-restore = gen-read + define, exactly the
          lossless-redefine pattern the 012 design documented by
          hand).

          FIRST APP CONSUMER: NOTES UNDO. The motivating case
          (design discussion 2026-09). Notes has ONE write funnel —
          every mutation ends at notes/doc/upsert over pure outline
          transforms — so app-level undo needs NO per-app storage:
            - notes/doc/upsert-in records nothing; the substrate
              keeps the before-image.
            - (undo (node-path 'note id)) in place of any history
              journal; the notes package keeps at most a UI-facing
              "undo the last change to THIS note" button wired to
              one per-path call.
            - Leaf-vs-whole is free: undo of one outline NODE and
              undo of the note SUBTREE are the same prim at two
              path granularities, because the outline is a subtree
              of tree leaves.
          The earlier candidate designs (JSON blob notes_history
          table; h.* prefix spine of copied subtrees) are SUPERSEDED
          and recorded here only so a future reader does not re-
          derive them — both were copy-based schemes working around
          a substrate that discarded before-images.

          FEEDS: local-first.sync (planned). Sync's merge base was
          already defined as (branch, generation) snapshot pins;
          with gen as a storage key, "common ancestor at pin" is a
          READ, not a snapshot artifact. The 017 counter's three
          uses — cache invalidation, merge base, and now storage
          key — are one counter. local-first's data-sync section
          gains one companion line: sync reconcile reads the data
          gen chain; it does not version data per-branch (that
          non-goal stands).

          FEEDS: habitat-events (planned note
          garden/design?slug=habitat-events-borg). Orthogonal by
          design: events answer OPERATIONS questions (who evaluated
          what form, when); tree-time answers STATE questions (what
          did the world look like). The two join on path+gen: an
          event row may reference the gen it wrote, making the
          audit trail (scoreboard: agent audit PARTIAL -> agent-
          readable by construction) navigable from event to state.
          "Stream not tree" stays right for events; this chapter is
          the tree half of the full Beads-equivalent picture:
          events for op-log, tree-time for versioned state, forge
          for intent — one merge algorithm across all three.

          SCOREBOARD EFFECT (beads-audit-gap): data state history
          moves NO -> YES-STRUCTURAL; time travel moves NO -> YES
          (the degenerate gen read).
     |))))

   (subsection gc
    (doc "Retention: gen-range partitions, jobs-runner rollup, policy as habitat data")
    (status planned)
    (implements image/lib/jobs.ml)
    (details
     (doc (|
          NO NEW SUBSYSTEM. Dolt ships dolt_gc because it owns its
          chunk store; here Postgres owns vacuum. GC is three
          existing pieces composed:

            - PARTITIONING: tree_versions is partitioned by gen
              range windows (e.g. 1000-gen ranges, created lazily).
              Physical removal is DROP PARTITION — instant, no
              vacuum scan. PG-native; the chunk-GC problem Dolt
              engineered against does not exist for a partitioned
              table.

            - RETENTION POLICY IS HABITAT DATA. Policy rows declare
              per-path-class retention (code paths: keep all
              history, it's small and it IS the audit trail; data
              paths: per-type policy, default keep-everything-until-
              a-policy-exists). habitat-events' "retention is
              habitat data (policy rows + rollup job on the
              existing jobs runner), never ad-hoc deletion under
              disk pressure" applies verbatim; the jobs runner
              (migration 015) executes.

            - ROLLUP, NOT SILENT LOSS. Before a window is dropped,
              surviving state collapses to a CHECKPOINT row at the
              window's boundary gen: the newest version of each
              path in the window is rewritten at the boundary, so
              reads as of ancient gens still resolve (to the
              checkpointed state) and head reads never notice.
              "Nobody gets everything forever — the audit claim
              survives at coarser grain."

          ORDERING: GC lands AFTER reads-as-of-gen and the
          consumers bed in. The first long pole is growth FROM
          code paths, which are tiny; habitat scale makes early
          compaction premature. The retention job is a TODO until
          a path class outgrows its welcome — measured via
          /timing-style storage accounting, not vibes.
     |))))

   (subsection coexistence-plan
    (doc "Phased, additive-only, flag-guarded; nothing existing is rewritten in place")
    (status planned)
    (details
     (doc (|
          PHASE 0 (this chapter): spec. No code.

          PHASE 1 — DUAL-WRITE SHADOW. New tree_versions table;
          write path appends version rows IN ADDITION to today's
          tree_nodes writes; a verify script compares versioned
          reads at head against today's reads across a sample
          corpus (the tree_verify precedent: sampling with
          env-controlled verbosity, never full-scan). Nothing
          reads the new table yet. This phase proves INSERT-only
          wire cost on a live habitat without consequence.

          PHASE 2 — READ SWITCH, FLAG-GUARDED. Reads gain the gen
          filter behind an env flag (the PP_TREE_* precedent:
          default off, exercise on dev, then flip). Head reads
          through the versioned table must return BIT-IDENTICAL
          results to the scaffolding mirrors — the projections
          subsection's equivalence verifier is the gate.
          code_history triggers keep firing until equivalence
          holds continuously on the live habitat for a soak
          window.

          PHASE 3 — CONSUMERS LIGHT UP. code-history / code-diff
          re-implemented over tree_versions (signatures unchanged);
          undo + gen-read + gen-diff prims land; notes undo UI
          ships as the proving app; 012 table to read-only
          archival, then drop.

          PHASE 4 — GC + CHAPTER COMPLETE. Partitioning + retention
          worker per gc subsection. This chapter's status moves to
          implemented when the retention worker runs on the jobs
          runner.

          ALL MIGRATIONS ADDITIVE per conventions (new table, new
          views, new columns — never rewrite payloads; 017's WHERE
          NOT EXISTS idempotence style). Downstream blast radius,
          audited in design: libs/* zero changes; scaffolding
          readers zero changes; habitat-events unaffected (append-
          only stream either way); local-first gains a read
          enhancement (merge base as read), no redesign.
     |))))

   (subsection non-goals
    (doc "What tree-time is NOT")
    (status planned)
    (details
     (doc (|
          - NOT branching data. The one-tree /data DECISION
            (single-head, user-owned, SPATIAL not TEMPORAL) stands
            at the CONCEPT level; this chapter adds TEMPORAL
            STORAGE-KEYS invisible to users — one head per path,
            many retained gens below it. The DECISION's reopen
            trigger (a user forking their own data to experiment
            and merge back) is a distinct feature; this chapter is
            compatible with it but does NOT build or reopen it.

          - NOT a Merkle-DAG / tamper-evident ledger. Hash-chained
            gens exist only if a future untrusted-writer scenario
            demands them (local-first's untrusted-client punt).
            Hashes here serve diff, not integrity.

          - NOT the habitat-events stream, and NOT replaced by it.
            Operations-audit and state-history answer different
            questions; one emissions table and one versioned tree.

          - NOT eval-scope or cross-path transaction undo as a
            storage primitive. (undo path) is per-path; session
            scope composes from habitat-events correlation later.

          - NOT a rewrite of libs/*, scaffolding tables, or the
            prim ABI. Optional args only; head-shape preserved;
            existing call sites mean exactly what they meant.

          - NOT content addressing. Physical dedup of identical
            bodies is deferred to one-tree.tree-model's existing
            "content-addressing later" note; append-only gives the
            structural sharing this design needs without it.

          - NOT rename-tracking. Path is part of the key; a rename
            is delete+create in history space. Same posture as git
            (detection over tracking); the blast-radius walking
            problem for historical names is accepted.
     |))))
  )))
